WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:13.080
AI security architecture protects the full system around the model: identity, data, prompts, retrieval, tools, memory, integrations, actions, logs, and governance.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.260
The model matters, but the surrounding architecture decides what the model can access and affect.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.160
A secure AI system is not created by prompt wording alone.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:28.540
It is created by boundaries, policies, validation, monitoring, and accountable ownership.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.680
Strong architectures separate trusted instructions from untrusted content.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.020
They enforce authorization in code and identity systems.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:46.320
They minimize data exposure, preserve source provenance, scope tools, validate arguments, monitor behavior, and require human approval for high-impact actions.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:52.940
They also make diagrams and logs clear enough that teams can understand both data flow and authority flow.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.960
AI systems should be useful, bounded, observable, and recoverable.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.060
Useful means they support real business goals.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.960
Bounded means access and actions are constrained by policy.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.380
Observable means teams can investigate what happened.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.200
Recoverable means failures can be contained, reversed where possible, and used to improve the system.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.780
These properties are architecture outcomes, not add-ons.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.400
The practical lesson is to design AI features like production systems with unusual inputs and powerful integrations.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.300
Identify what the model can read, remember, decide, and do.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.240
Protect sensitive data before it enters the model.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.900
Keep permissions narrow.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.280
Treat tools as security boundaries.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.340
Monitor after deployment.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.240
Align every design choice with organizational risk decisions.
